// Nouns describes each noun the methods' commands have.
func Nouns() []apicli.Noun {
	return []apicli.Noun{
		{Name: "api-tokens", Short: "Create, list or revoke API tokens", Long: "TokenService manages API tokens: secrets for scripts, the CLI and integrations,\neach acting for the person who made it, within a scope. See\ndocs/adr/0016-scoped-api-tokens.md.\n\nSend a token in the Authorization header, as \"Bearer dwt_…\", with REST, Connect,\ngRPC or gRPC-Web alike. A read-only token may call the methods whose effect is\nEFFECT_READ; a read-write token may also call EFFECT_PLAN and EFFECT_APPLY\nmethods. No token may call an EFFECT_DESTRUCTIVE method, AuthService, or this\nservice: they need a signed-in session."},
		{Name: "auth-state", Short: "Say whether the install has an owner", Long: "The install's auth state says whether it has an owner yet, who is signed in on a\nrequest, and which address passkeys are made for there. dockwell auth-state get\nneeds no sign-in."},
		{Name: "changes", Short: "Propose, get, list, apply, watch, undo or discard changes", Long: "A change is a set of edits to managed state, with the plan that carries them\nout. You propose edits and get a plan back; applying it writes immutable\nrevisions and runs its steps on the servers; undoing it makes a new plan that\nputs things back. A change applies only as it was planned, and a plan never\nshows a secret, only that it changed.\n\nSee docs/adr/0009-resources-revisions-and-plans.md and\ndocs/adr/0017-the-change-api-and-the-agent-stream.md."},
		{Name: "claim-link", Short: "Check, begin or finish claim link", Long: "A claim link, which dockwell run and dockwell claim print, creates the install's\nowner with a passkey, and works once. dockwell claim-link check describes the\nserver a link claims, and dockwell claim-link begin and dockwell claim-link\nfinish make the passkey, which takes a browser: open the link in one."},
		{Name: "drift", Short: "List, revert or adopt drift", Long: "Drift is a difference between what a server has and the latest revision of a\nresource it should have, as when someone changed it by hand. dockwell drift list\nasks each connected server's agent, and changes nothing. dockwell drift revert\nplans putting the revision back, and dockwell drift adopt plans keeping what the\nserver has as a new revision: each is a change, with a plan to apply."},
		{Name: "passkeys", Short: "List, begin add, finish add or remove passkeys", Long: "Passkeys are how you sign in to the panel. A browser, a phone or a security key\nmakes each one, for one address, and only it can use it. Adding and removing\nthem needs a signed-in session, which no API token gives, so you do it in the\npanel, under Profile and security."},
		{Name: "servers", Short: "List, get or watch servers", Long: "ServerService lists the servers Dockwell manages and streams their live state,\nas each server's agent reports it.\n\ndockwell servers watch has no REST path: REST has no way to stream messages, so\na REST client polls dockwell servers list or dockwell servers get instead, and\nanything that wants the stream opens it with Connect, gRPC or gRPC-Web. See\ndocs/adr/0015-rest-and-openapi-from-the-schema.md."},
		{Name: "sessions", Short: "List or revoke sessions, and 6 more", Long: "A session is a browser you're signed in on. Signing in takes a passkey, a code\nfrom an authenticator app, a recovery code, or a one-time link from dockwell\nrecover, and sets a session cookie. No API token can sign in, or list or end\nsessions: how you sign in changes only by signing in, in the panel."},
		{Name: "system", Short: "Return the build of the control plane that answers", Long: "SystemService describes the Dockwell install itself. It needs no sign-in, so the\nCLI, agents and other tools can check what they are talking to first."},
		{Name: "totp", Short: "Get, disable or unlock TOTP, and 3 more", Long: "Code sign-in lets you sign in with a code from an authenticator app, or with a\none-time recovery code, beside your passkeys. Wrong codes slow it down, and too\nmany lock it until dockwell totp unlock turns it back on. Setting it up needs a\nsigned-in session, which no API token gives, so you do it in the panel, under\nProfile and security."},
	}
}

// Kinds describes the commands for each kind of resource an edit's spec
